A block-compressed (DXT1/23/45) texture never samples a render surface; plus a diagnostic alpha dump (LD_DUMP_ALPHA).
Live draw's bind loop matched a texture to a colour surface by address alone (and to its own target through the
feedback copy), whatever the texture's format. No RSX colour surface format is block-compressed, so on the console a
DXT texture at a surface's address reads the bytes there as blocks. ToD binds a 16x16 DXT1 at VRAM offset 0 (the
display buffer 0:0x0, 1280x720/704 surfaces) as its "no texture" unit: unit 2 of 562 scene draws of PSO 2e66e764
(Fastoon spaceport, tods-gfx-cap3 frame 10016), unit 3 of the bloom chain and composite, unit 7 elsewhere. PSO
2e66e764's program writes the colour output's alpha from that unit's blue, and the bloom bright pass (PSO 3c988e50)
weights its taps by pow(alpha * c2.x, c3.y): with the whole display surface bound, the scene alpha was the last
frame's blue, so the purple floor, sky and walls bloomed (composite 78ede0c4: dst += bloom * 0.8). Materials that map
the unit across their faces showed the last frame itself, HUD included, rotated 180 degrees: the "Enter Ship" prompt on
the spaceport wall (tods-gfx-scn: it appears between scene draws 280 and 320 into 0x870000). On the console the 128
bytes at offset 0 are row 0 of the display buffer, which the frame's first draw (constant 0,0,0,0 quad, PSO 0f46b12b)
zeroes: DXT1 black, alpha 1. The guest bytes there are 0 in the port too (tods-gfx-post draw-inputs u03.bin).
Now such a texture skips the colour-surface match, the zeta alias and the self-read copy and is decoded from the guest
bytes. LD_DXT_SURF_ALIAS=1 = old behaviour (A/B); "[dxt-alias]" lines log the first 8 refused aliases. Upstream
ps3recomp PR #508 (merged 2026-10-26) makes the same rule for rsx_draw_engine.c; live draw had not got it.
Diagnostic: LD_DUMP_ALPHA=1 writes <dump>.a.pgm next to every 8-bit surface dump (LD_SURF_DUMP, LD_DRAW_DUMP).
Host test: R&C port/rc/tests/run_dxt_alias_test.sh.

